l prize in physics  for the finding so really briefly we're  going to have another discussion of the  wu experiment later on  what she did here is she studied a  global sixth cobalt 60 decay into nickel  beta decays and what she was able to do  experimentally  with the magnetic field align the cobalt  60  and then just count the number of  electrons coming out and it turns out  that  number of electrons coming out on the  bottom and the top are not the same  she did this by reverting the magnetic  field and the  experimental data is shown here you see  as a function of time so you  you know you just look at counting  weights as a function of time after  injecting the probe  um and you revert you do the measurement  by reverting the magnetic field  you see very clearly here that there is  an asymmetry  in the better decays and that asymmetry  immediately tells you that there has to  be  parity violation in better decays  or in weak interaction so she found  that this uh specific picture here  bioli violates uh parity and this is a  dramatic signature of the read  interaction and we'll let us see how we  can actually understand this note that  parity is conserved in electromagnetic  interaction in the strong interaction  one more word on parity invasion an  inversion you can define a parity  operator  and if you apply this parity operator  for example on a vector  with x y and z components you simply  turn the direction over here with a  minus sign  you

